Explain stack vs heap memory. When would you prefer one over the other? What are the trade-offs?

Strong Staff-Level Answer
High-level:
Stack → automatic, LIFO, fast allocation/deallocation
Heap → dynamic, flexible, slower, managed manually (or via allocators)

Key Differences
Aspect	                                Stack	                                                     Heap
Allocation	                      Compile-time / function call	                                   Runtime
Speed	                                 Very fast	                                                 Slower
Size	                                 Limited	                                                   Large
Lifetime	                            Scope-based	                                                 Controlled manually
Fragmentation	                          No	                                                        Yes


When to Use
Use stack:
Small, short-lived objects
Deterministic lifetime
Performance-critical paths
Use heap:
Large objects
Dynamic size
Shared ownership

🔹 Staff-Level Insight (Important)
“In high-performance systems, I default to stack allocation unless there is a clear need for heap—because 
heap introduces fragmentation, cache misses, and allocator contention.”


🔥 Follow-up Questions
Q: What happens on stack overflow?
→ Program crashes (segmentation fault)
Q: Why is stack faster?
→ Simple pointer increment/decrement (no bookkeeping)
Q: How does heap affect cache locality?
→ Poor locality → more cache misses → slower

🧠 1. Stack vs Heap — Deep Grilling

❓ Q1
If stack is faster, why don’t we just allocate everything on the stack?
✅ Answer
Stack size is limited (typically a few MB per thread)
No support for dynamic lifetime (objects tied to scope)
Cannot return references to stack memory safely
Deep recursion risks stack overflow
👉 Staff insight:
“Stack is ideal for deterministic lifetimes, but heap is necessary for dynamic and cross-scope ownership.”
🔥 What they test
Practical constraints, not theory


❓ Q2
How does stack allocation work at assembly level?
✅ Answer
Stack pointer (rsp in x86_64) moves
Allocation = decrement rsp
Deallocation = increment rsp
👉 Example:
sub rsp, 32   ; allocate
add rsp, 32   ; deallocate
👉 No fragmentation, no metadata

🔥 What they test
Low-level understanding (system engineer expectation)
❓ Q3
Can heap allocation ever be faster than stack?
✅ Answer
Yes, in some cases:
Custom allocators (memory pools, slab allocators)
Reusing memory → avoids repeated allocation cost
Thread-local allocators reduce contention
👉 Example:
High-performance servers use memory pools instead of stack
🔥 What they test
Ability to break assumptions


