🧠 3. RAII (VERY IMPORTANT)
❓ Question
What is RAII? Why is it important in C++?
✅ Strong Answer
RAII = Resource Acquisition Is Initialization
Resource is acquired in constructor
Released in destructor

class File {
public:
    File() { open(); }
    ~File() { close(); }
};

🔹 Why Important?
Prevents leaks
Exception safe
Deterministic cleanup
🔥 Staff-Level Insight
“RAII is the foundation of modern C++ memory safety. Smart pointers, locks, file handlers—all rely on RAII to guarantee cleanup 
even during exceptions.”

❓ Follow-up
Q: How does RAII help in multithreading?
👉 Example:
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
Automatically unlocks when scope ends

🧠 3. RAII — Deep Grilling
❓ Q8
Can RAII fail? Give real scenarios.
✅ Answer
Yes:
Cyclic references (shared_ptr)
Destructor never called
Program crash (SIGKILL)
Destructors not executed
Manual resource mismanagement
Raw pointers bypass RAII

🔥 Staff Insight
“RAII guarantees cleanup only if destructors run—so system crashes still require external recovery mechanisms.”
🔥 What they test
Edge-case realism

❓ Q9
How does RAII ensure exception safety?
✅ Answer
Stack unwinding calls destructors automatically
Resources released even if exception thrown
👉 Example:
void f() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
    throw std::runtime_error("error");
}
→ mutex still unlocked
🔥 What they test
Exception model understanding
❓ Q10
Can RAII be used for non-memory resources?
✅ Answer
Yes:
File handles
Sockets
Mutex locks
Database connections
👉 Example:
std::lock_guard
std::fstream
🔥 What they test
Concept generalization

