🧠 4. Rule of 5
❓ Question
Explain the Rule of 5. Why is it needed?
✅ Strong Answer
If your class manages resources, define:
Destructor
Copy constructor
Copy assignment
Move constructor
Move assignment
🔹 Why?
Prevent double free
Handle deep copy
Enable efficient moves
🔹 Example
class Buffer {
    int* data;
public:
    Buffer(size_t size) { data = new int[size]; }

    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }

    Buffer(const Buffer& other) {
        data = new int[...]; // deep copy
    }

    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
        data = other.data;
        other.data = nullptr;
    }
};

🔥 Staff-Level Insight
“Move semantics are critical for performance—especially in systems dealing with large buffers or real-time constraints.”
❓ Follow-up
Q: What happens if you don’t define them?
→ Compiler generates shallow copies → bugs

🧠 4. Rule of 5 — Deep Grilling
❓ Q11
When would you delete copy constructor?
✅ Answer
Unique ownership semantics

class Resource {
public:
    Resource(const Resource&) = delete;
};

👉 Example:
File descriptor
Mutex
🔥 What they test
Ownership modeling
❓ Q12
Why must move constructor set source to nullptr?
✅ Answer
Prevent double free
other.data = nullptr;

👉 Without this:
Both objects free same memory → crash
🔥 What they test
Memory safety depth
❓ Q13 (Very Hard)
What happens if move constructor throws?
✅ Answer
Should not throw (noexcept)
Otherwise:
STL containers fallback to copy
Performance degradation
👉 Staff insight:
“Move operations must be noexcept for optimal container performance.”

🔥 What they test
STL internals understanding


